Reason:
For GPIO banks without PWM support, `mvchip->mvpwm` is `NULL`. The original condition only checks whether the PWM subsystem is compiled in (`IS_REACHABLE(CONFIG_PWM)`), but does not verify that the bank actually has PWM functionality. As a result, `mvebu_pwm_suspend()` and `mvebu_pwm_resume()` are called with a NULL `mvpwm` pointer, causing a NULL pointer dereference when the functions attempt to access `mvpwm->blink_select` (via regmap operations).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
if (IS_REACHABLE(CONFIG_PWM) && mvchip->mvpwm)
    mvebu_pwm_suspend(mvchip);
</text>
<text>
if (IS_REACHABLE(CONFIG_PWM) && mvchip->mvpwm)
    mvebu_pwm_resume(mvchip);
</text>
Reason:
Adding the `&& mvchip->mvpwm` check ensures that the PWM suspend/resume functions are only called when the GPIO bank actually has PWM support (i.e., `mvpwm` is not NULL). This prevents the NULL pointer dereference without affecting the behavior for banks that do support PWM.

